Upstream-Status: Inappropriate

Subject: [PATCH] x86/mm: Flush all TLBs when freeing kernel page tables

pud_free_pmd_page() and pmd_free_pte_page() free page tables and expect
flush_tlb_kernel_range() on the first page to drop the paging-structure
caches.  That only holds today because the INVLPGB variant ignores the
address and flushes everything; without INVLPGB it misses inactive PCIDs,
and once the INVLPGB flush is limited to the range it misses the tables
under the rest of the PUD.  Jann Horn reported both.

Use flush_tlb_all(), which is what the INVLPGB case does now in effect.
These paths only run when a huge vmap/ioremap mapping replaces smaller
ones.
---
--- a/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
@@ -745,8 +745,8 @@ int pud_free_pmd_page(pud_t *pud, unsigned long addr)
 
 	pud_clear(pud);
 
-	/* INVLPG to clear all paging-structure caches */
-	flush_tlb_kernel_range(addr, addr + PAGE_SIZE-1);
+	/* Drop paging-structure caches for all the freed tables */
+	flush_tlb_all();
 
 	for (i = 0; i < PTRS_PER_PMD; i++) {
 		if (!pmd_none(pmd_sv[i])) {
@@ -777,8 +777,8 @@ int pmd_free_pte_page(pmd_t *pmd, unsigned long addr)
 	pt = page_ptdesc(pmd_page(*pmd));
 	pmd_clear(pmd);
 
-	/* INVLPG to clear all paging-structure caches */
-	flush_tlb_kernel_range(addr, addr + PAGE_SIZE-1);
+	/* Drop paging-structure caches for the freed table */
+	flush_tlb_all();
 
 	pagetable_dtor_free(pt);
 
